Parent: 

To design a group-fair count-min sketch that is fair for all groups, we propose two novel techniques based on the following observation: if elements from different groups do not collide with each other in the same row of the sketch, then their frequency estimates are guaranteed to be equal. In this paper, 

We will design a group-fair count-min sketch by partitioning and assigning the columns of the sketch to two distinct groups g1andg2by strategically partitioning them using semi-uniform hash functions. This approach ensures that elements from different groups do not collide with each other in at least one row, thus eliminating the possibility of disproport
Parent: 

To design a group-fair count-min sketch that is fair for all groups, we propose two novel techniques based on the following observation: if high-frequency elements can collide with low-frequency ones, adding a major overestimation to their counts. Specifically, we ensure our goal by designing group-aware 

We will now present and analyze three sketches that satisfy Definition 2 (group fairness) for any number of groups g1andg2(Section 4). We first introduce the concept of semi-uniform hash functions in Section 3. Then, we formally define our problem of interest: to design a group-fair count-min sketch whose overall additive error increase compared to CM (price of fairness) is small.
